<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. The script loads
    GCC 15.2 x86-64 shared objects from tests/fixtures/got-slot-imports/
    (sources and recipe there). calls.c is built -fno-plt, so each call and
    tail jump goes through the import's GLOB_DAT slot. viaplt.c calls the same
    imports through the PLT.

    shared_x86_64.so links both objects, so GNU ld gives free, strlen, memcmp
    and exit a .plt.got stub that jumps through the same slot. elfgotimports
    registers the slot as a FunctionSymbol of the import's name, and a call
    through the slot resolves to it. The library prototype reached the slot
    only by name, and a by-name park lands on the first FunctionSymbol of the
    name: the stub. So the calls through the slot printed free(), strlen()
    and memcmp(). A no-return fact reached the slot by no route at all, so
    check's exit(2) ran into the next function there, and into unmapped
    memory in noplt_x86_64.so, which has no .plt* section. The import facts
    keyed by entry address are now also emitted at the slot.

    Controls: the PLT callers (drop, len2, bail) and the no-PLT image's calls
    to free and strlen were already right. A last load of shared_x86_64.so
    with importdata off registers no symbol at the slots, so the slot facts
    park nowhere: the calls through the slots stay unnamed, as on main, and
    the no-return fact falls back by name to the exit stub, which bail's call
    already reaches. A strict fix with one pass.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">c3</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
